{"candidates":[{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"The government maintained a broadly neutral fiscal stance in 2022, targeting a fiscal deficit of 3.8 percent of GDP.","claim_type":"source_assertion","polarity":"context","actor":"The government","action":"maintained a broadly neutral fiscal stance","mechanism":"Targeted a fiscal deficit of 3.8 percent of GDP.","outcome":"The fiscal deficit target was 3.8 percent of GDP.","dates":["2022"],"quantities":["3.8 percent of GDP"],"excerpt":"The government has maintained a broadly neutral fiscal stance in 2022, targeting a fiscal deficit of 3.8 percent of GDP.","locator":"page 2","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Directly within the target year 2022.","source_limitations":["This is an IMF Executive Board press-release summary, not an independent audit.","The passage characterizes the fiscal stance and target rather than documenting final execution."],"question_ids":["5B.1","5B.3"],"verification_status":"corrected","verification_notes":["Removed the unsupported causal outcome that fiscal policy kept the economy neutral while it recovered."],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"The authorities used largely untargeted subsidies to mitigate the rise in the cost of living, offsetting windfall gains from oil revenues.","claim_type":"source_assertion","polarity":"mixed","actor":"The authorities","action":"used largely untargeted subsidies","mechanism":"The subsidies were used to mitigate rising living costs and offset windfall gains from oil revenues.","outcome":"The measures were intended to mitigate the rise in the cost of living.","dates":["2022"],"quantities":[],"excerpt":"It has used largely untargeted subsidies to mitigate the rise in the cost of living, offsetting the windfall gains from oil revenues.","locator":"page 2","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Directly within the target year 2022.","source_limitations":["This is an IMF Executive Board press-release summary.","The passage does not establish household-level incidence, targeting precision, or the realized effect on inflation."],"question_ids":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.9"],"verification_status":"corrected","verification_notes":["Removed the quantity "around 2 percent of GDP," which does not appear in the cited excerpt.","Recast the outcome as an intended mitigation of living-cost increases rather than a demonstrated reduction in inflation pressure."],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"The authorities smoothed retail fuel prices and took steps to stabilize the prices of some essential food items, including a nonbinding agreement covering 24 items initially for six months.","claim_type":"source_assertion","polarity":"mixed","actor":"The authorities","action":"smoothed retail fuel prices and took steps to stabilize some essential food prices","mechanism":"The measures included a nonbinding agreement with food producers and distributors, a temporary tariff reduction on certain foods, and steps to reduce distribution costs and promote food production.","outcome":"Several food producers and distributors agreed to stabilize the prices of 24 items initially for six months.","dates":["2022"],"quantities":["24 items","six months"],"excerpt":"As global oil prices have risen, the authorities have smoothed retail fuel prices and took steps to stabilize prices of some essential food items. In a nonbinding agreement, several food producers and distributors agreed to stabilize the prices of 24 items initially for six months. In addition, the authorities provided a temporary tariff reduction on certain foods, while taking steps to reduce distribution costs and promote food production, especially by small farmers.","locator":"page 12","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 inflation-response period.","source_limitations":["IMF staff summary, not a direct administrative order.","The passage does not establish household incidence or demonstrate that the measures lowered fuel or food cost pressures."],"question_ids":["5B.1","5B.6","5B.8","5B.9"],"verification_status":"corrected","verification_notes":["Changed the outcome from a demonstrated lowering of cost pressures to the agreement expressly reported in the passage.","Preserved the nonbinding status of the food-price agreement."],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"Compared with the authorities' estimated 2022 outturn, expenditure in the 2023 draft budget was 8.5 percent higher in nominal terms and accommodated higher interest payments, social pensions for the elderly, priority infrastructure projects, and selected rural support programs.","claim_type":"source_assertion","polarity":"context","actor":"The authorities","action":"proposed higher expenditure in the 2023 draft budget","mechanism":"The proposed increase accommodated interest payments, non-contributory social pensions for the elderly, priority infrastructure projects, fertilizers, national milk procurement, and rural supplies.","outcome":"Planned expenditure was 8.5 percent higher in nominal terms than the authorities' estimated 2022 outturn, or 0.1 percentage points higher as a share of the authorities' projected GDP.","dates":["2023 draft budget","2022 outturn"],"quantities":["8.5 percent","0.1 percentage points of authorities' projected GDP"],"excerpt":"Compared to the authorities estimated 2022 outturn, expenditure in 2023 is increased by 8.5 percent in nominal terms (or by 0.1 percentage points of authorities’ projected GDP). This increase accommodates higher spending of interest payments, some social programs—particularly (non-contributory) social pensions for the elderly—and priority infrastructure projects. In addition, the allotted funding for social programs related to fertilizers, procurement of national milk, and rural supplies has also been increased, though the size of the programs remains modest.","locator":"page 13","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Indirectly relevant to 2022 because it compares the immediately following draft budget with the estimated 2022 outturn.","source_limitations":["This concerns proposed allocations in a draft budget, not executed spending.","The rural programs were described as remaining modest, and the passage does not assess implementation quality."],"question_ids":["5B.1","5B.6"],"verification_status":"corrected","verification_notes":["Changed the polarity from favorable to context because the passage reports proposed allocations without evaluating their results.","Clarified that the expenditure increase was proposed in a draft budget rather than implemented."],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"Retail fuel price stabilization came at a sizeable budgetary cost and disproportionately benefited higher-income households.","claim_type":"source_assertion","polarity":"adverse","actor":"Retail fuel price stabilization","action":"stabilized retail fuel prices","mechanism":"The measure incurred a sizeable budgetary cost.","outcome":"The estimated cost was around 1.5 percent of GDP, and the benefits disproportionately accrued to higher-income households.","dates":["2022"],"quantities":["around 1.5 percent of GDP"],"excerpt":"The measure has come at a sizeable budgetary cost, estimated at around 1.5 percent of GDP, and has disproportionately benefited higher income households.","locator":"page 19","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Directly within the target year 2022.","source_limitations":["This is an IMF staff estimate and distributional assessment.","The excerpt does not provide the underlying household-level distribution or survey methodology."],"question_ids":["5B.8","5B.9"],"verification_status":"accepted","verification_notes":["The claim, quantity, attribution, and distributional qualification are directly supported by the excerpt."],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"LAW-005","claim":"For 10 to 16 December 2022, the fuel stimulus was set at 26.96 percent for gasoline under 91 octanes, 0.00 percent for gasoline of at least 91 octanes and non-fossil fuels, and 59.77 percent for diesel.","claim_type":"legal_status","polarity":"mixed","actor":"Secretaría de Hacienda y Crédito Público","action":"set the fuel-stimulus percentages for the specified period","mechanism":"Established the applicable IEPS stimulus percentages by fuel category.","outcome":"The agreement established the stated stimulus schedule for 10 to 16 December 2022.","dates":["10 al 16 de diciembre de 2022"],"quantities":["26.96%","0.00%","59.77%"],"excerpt":"Gasolina menor a 91 octanos 26.96%
Gasolina mayor o igual a 91 octanos y combustibles no fósiles 0.00%
Diésel 59.77%","locator":"extracted HTML block 2","attribution":"Secretaría de Hacienda y Crédito Público, Acuerdo 173/2022","period_fit":"Directly within the target year 2022.","source_limitations":["The legal schedule does not establish realized pass-through to retail prices or actual taxpayer incidence.","The excerpt does not establish who benefited from the stimulus."],"question_ids":["5B.3","5B.9"],"verification_status":"corrected","verification_notes":["Removed monetary amounts not present in the exact excerpt.","Removed the publication date, which is not present in the exact excerpt.","Replaced the outcome that taxes were reduced with the narrower legal finding that the agreement established the applicable schedule."],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"In April 2021, the authorities enacted legislation restricting workforce subcontracting with the stated aim of preventing violations of labor law and tax evasion.","claim_type":"legal_status","polarity":"context","actor":"The authorities","action":"enacted legislation restricting workforce subcontracting","mechanism":"The law banned service firms from supplying employees supervised and managed by a third party, retained specified forms of service subcontracting, tightened registration and reporting requirements, and made non-compliance a criminal offence.","outcome":"Non-compliance with the new subcontracting law became a criminal offence.","dates":["April 2021"],"quantities":[],"excerpt":"In April 2021, the authorities enacted new legislation restricting the subcontracting of the workforce, with the aim of preventing the violation of labor law and tax evasion. The new law bans service firms from providing employees to be supervised and managed by a third-party, while it still allows subcontracting of services if the employees are monitored by the firm providing the service. The legislation also tightened registration and reporting requirements. Non-compliance with this new subcontracting law became a criminal offence.","locator":"page 69","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Antecedent reform with relevance to the 2022 assessment.","source_limitations":["The passage describes the law and its stated aim, not its incidence, enforcement, or effectiveness.","The passage does not establish employment outcomes."],"question_ids":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.8"],"verification_status":"corrected","verification_notes":["Changed the polarity from favorable to context because enactment and stated aims do not establish beneficial results.","Removed the employment-transition outcome, which is not supported by this exact excerpt.","Preserved the stated aim as an aim rather than a demonstrated effect."],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"In manufacturing and retail trade, the subcontracting reform was associated with sharp reductions in subcontracted employment and no apparent change in total employment by the end of the third quarter of 2021.","claim_type":"source_assertion","polarity":"favorable","actor":"The subcontracting reform","action":"was associated with changes in employment classification in manufacturing and retail trade","mechanism":"The passage suggests that most previously subcontracted workers in those sectors were rehired as direct employees.","outcome":"Compared with the first quarter of 2021, subcontracted employment fell by 87 percent in manufacturing and 70 percent in retail trade, while total employment in those sectors showed no apparent change by the end of the third quarter.","dates":["first quarter of 2021","end of the third quarter of 2021"],"quantities":["87 percent","70 percent"],"excerpt":"In the manufacturing and retail trade sectors, for example, subcontracted employment was reduced by 87 percent and 70 percent, respectively, compared to the first quarter of 2021. At the same time, there was no apparent change in total employment in these sectors by the end of the third quarter, suggesting that most of the previously subcontracted workers were rehired as direct employees.","locator":"page 69","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Antecedent reform evidence used in the 2022 IMF assessment.","source_limitations":["The evidence is limited to manufacturing and retail trade and does not establish a nationwide employment effect.","The rehiring explanation is presented as a suggestion rather than a confirmed causal finding."],"question_ids":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.8"],"verification_status":"corrected","verification_notes":["Corrected the locator from page 70 to the exact supplied unit locator, page 69.","Narrowed "aggregate employment" to total employment in the two sectors identified in the excerpt.","Preserved the source's associative and suggestive wording rather than asserting causation."],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"Under the current administration, Mexico's revenue-mobilization efforts centered on improving tax compliance through a series of reforms introduced since 2019.","claim_type":"source_assertion","polarity":"favorable","actor":"The current administration","action":"centered revenue-mobilization efforts on improving tax compliance","mechanism":"Reforms included restricting offsets of excess tax credits, requiring digital platforms to withhold taxes, implementing General Anti-Abuse Rules, prohibiting company mergers for tax purposes, and enacting OECD BEPS actions 2, 4, and 12.","outcome":"The cited reforms altered tax-compliance and anti-avoidance rules.","dates":["since 2019"],"quantities":[],"excerpt":"Under the current administration, revenue mobilization efforts in Mexico have centered on improving tax compliance. Notable reforms since 2019 have included:
• The abolition of the right to offset excess tax credits against other taxes, building on staff recommendations regarding tackling large-scale tax fraud;
• The requirement that digital platforms withhold taxes due on transactions in which they acted as intermediary;
• Implementation of General Anti-Abuse Rules (GAAR) and the prohibition of company mergers for tax purposes to tackle corporate tax planning; and
• Enaction of actions 2, 4 and 12 in the OECD’s Base Erosion and Profit Shifting (BEPS) agenda.","locator":"page 90","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Directly relevant to 2022 fiscal administration and its recent antecedents.","source_limitations":["The excerpt identifies reforms but does not quantify their revenue effects or prove improved compliance outcomes.","This is not evidence of enforcement against any particular politically connected actor."],"question_ids":["5B.3","5B.5","5B.7"],"verification_status":"corrected","verification_notes":["Changed the action from the achieved result "improved tax compliance administration" to the supported description of where efforts were centered.","Removed revenue-growth and VAT-evasion outcomes, dates, and quantities that appear elsewhere on the page but not in the exact excerpt."],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"The IMF informational annex stated that comprehensive economic data for Mexico were available on a timely basis and adequate for surveillance, and that Mexico subscribed to the SDDS.","claim_type":"source_assertion","polarity":"favorable","actor":"Mexico","action":"made comprehensive economic data available and subscribed to the SDDS","mechanism":"Economic data were made available on a timely basis within the IMF surveillance and dissemination framework.","outcome":"The IMF assessed the economic data as adequate for surveillance.","dates":["as of August 31, 2022"],"quantities":[],"excerpt":"Mexico has accepted the obligations of Article VIII, sections 2, 3, and 4. Comprehensive economic data are available for Mexico on a timely basis and economic data are adequate for surveillance. It subscribes to the SDDS.","locator":"page 97","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 surveillance period.","source_limitations":["This is an IMF surveillance assessment rather than a measurement of every dimension of statistical quality.","The statement does not evaluate every dataset or statistical series individually."],"question_ids":["5B.7"],"verification_status":"accepted","verification_notes":["The claim accurately attributes the assessment to the IMF and is directly supported by the excerpt."],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"The IMF projected that Mexico's public debt and gross financing needs would increase somewhat over the main projection period and decline over the extended 10-year period.","claim_type":"source_assertion","polarity":"context","actor":"Mexico's public sector","action":"was projected to follow an initially rising and subsequently declining debt and gross-financing-needs path","mechanism":"The IMF assessed the projected path over its main and extended horizons.","outcome":"The IMF judged the debt sustainable with high probability over the extended time horizon.","dates":[],"quantities":["extended 10-year period","high probability"],"excerpt":"The projected debt path and GFN are expected to increase somewhat over the main projection period but decline over the extended 10-year period. Debt is therefore judged to be sustainable with high probability over the extended time horizon.","locator":"page 60","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"A forward-looking debt-sustainability assessment issued in 2022.","source_limitations":["This is an IMF projection and risk assessment, not an ex post outcome.","The assessment depends on the model, horizon, and underlying fiscal and macroeconomic assumptions."],"question_ids":["5B.3","5B.10"],"verification_status":"corrected","verification_notes":["Replaced "broadly stable in the medium term" with the excerpt's statement that debt and gross financing needs were expected to increase somewhat over the main projection period.","Removed unsupported numerical debt ratios and calendar years from the candidate.","Removed the unsupported mechanism asserting that fiscal surpluses would offset debt dynamics."],"premium_verification_required":false}],"rejected_or_omitted_passages":[],"unresolved_gaps":["No direct 2022 evidence in the supplied packet for 5B.2 on appointment, independence, or performance-based retention of qualified economic professionals."]}
